rt of using the term  altruistic it's because there are many  different motivations even for people  who are paid is that the main reason  well exactly I think the we have this  assumption that if you're paid it can't  be altruistic but when I interviewed egg  donors and sperm donors they were being  paid and yet they also felt that they  were helping people so part of what I  try to do in the book is collapse this  distinction that the world is not neat  and tidy where you know the altruist  soar over here and the paid folks are  over here and so I think when we look at  how people actually exist in the world  and how they think about that the money  that they're paid it causes us to  question those categories great now in  fact now too  unpaid better term than altruistic  unpaid egg providers you're gonna say  something about them yeah so I think  that where I've heard people talk about  egg donors who aren't paid it typically  is people's friends or sisters or  cousins who might be willing to provide  them an egg in order to reduce the costs  of their egg donation expenses within  family donation I've also heard reports  of it happening in sperm donors I don't  know that I've seen a systematic study  of these kinds of experiences but the  questions that people raise are if  you're getting genetic material from  somebody in your own family how is that  going to then shape the family dynamics  going forward and are people going to  treat each other differently how is the  child gonna think about who its parent  is so those are some of the questions  that get raised with unpaid within  family donation right so the idea that  you might be both genetic mother and  sociological and/or family and writing  how to kind of put those two together is  fascinating and I would say that this is  more of a concern for same-sex couples  like lesbian couples so because lesbians  don't have some of the same protections  in law in terms of their relationships  with their children a 